  "query_preview": "..(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 505 AM EDT THU AUG 27 2026\n\nThe most current model data continues suggest the current forecast\nis on track for a dry and gradually warming period for Kentucky \nfrom this weekend and beyond with low 90s on tap for highs to \nstart the new work week. We still see the potential for convection\nin the south on Tuesday and for more of the area on Wednesday, \nbut it is certainly not a slam dunk at this point. \n\nThe previous long term discussion follows:\n\nEastern Kentucky enters the long term forecast period on the back \nside of the upper level trough axis responsible for Thursday's \nconvective activity. A weak secondary boundary situated within the \nresultant northwesterly flow regime will push through the CWA on \nFriday, but with a drier airmass actively advecting into the column \naloft, this is not expected to yield any substantial sensible \nweather impacts. Expect partly cloudy skies, a shift to north-\nnortheasterly surface winds, and slightly cooler conditions (highs \nin the low to mid 80s) across the forecast area to wrap up the work \nweek. Ridge-valley temperature splits and river valley fog formation \nis plausible for Friday night into Saturday morning, as post-frontal \nhigh pressure is progged to build into the forecast area this \nweekend. \n\nUnder the influence of that surface high pressure system, expect \nmostly clear conditions to prevail on Saturday and Sunday. Sensible \nweather conditions this weekend will be diurnally modulated, with \ncumulus clouds developing in the afternoon hours and continued \nvalley fog formation at night. Temperatures will gradually warm \nthroughout the weekend though, as winds will veer back towards a \nmore southerly orientation by Sunday. Saturday's highs in the mid to \nupper 80s accordingly trend up a few degrees on Sunday before \napproaching the 90s early next week. \n\nConfidence remains high that a warmer and drier pattern will remain \nin place across the region early next week. Forecast guidance \ncollectively resolves mid/upper level ridging across the Ohio River \nValley on Monday and Tuesday. Modeled 850mb temps near/above 20 \ndegrees Celsius in place favors high temperatures in the lower 90s \nat the surface, but the blended model guidance used to populate the \nlong term forecast grids is likely a little bit too warm/moist. \nDiurnally mixed dewpoints will likely under-perform NBM guidance and \nremain in the low to mid 60s. This should prevent heat indices from \nclimbing into the triple digits. While it will feel seasonably hot \noutside on these two days, heat headlines should not be needed.\n\nThat upper level ridge looks to broaden out by midweek, with a \nseries of disturbances expected to propagate around its flattened \nnorthern periphery. The PoPs related to these disturbances have \nactually trended downward relative to previous forecast packages, \nwith only slight chances for isolated convection included in \nTuesday's forecast grids. Slightly better rain chances arrive ahead \nof a stronger disturbance and cold front on Wednesday, although that \nsystem's specific forecast details are masked by the increase in \nmodel spread at that temporal range. Despite that uncertainty, the \nmidweek increase in PoPs should work to provide some relief from the \nearly week heat. In short, temperatures should trend back towards \nclimatological norms as we transition from August into September.",
